NNAAMMEE
     ccuu — serial terminal emulator

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ccuu [--ddrr] [--EE _e_s_c_a_p_e___c_h_a_r] [--ll _l_i_n_e] [--ss _s_p_e_e_d | --_s_p_e_e_d]
     ccuu [_h_o_s_t]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     ccuu is used to connect to another system over a serial link.  In the era
     before modern networks, it was typically used to connect to a modem in
     order to dial in to a remote host.  It is now frequently used for tasks
     such as attaching to the serial console of another machine for adminis‐
     trative or debugging purposes.

     The options are as follows:

     --dd    Specify that the line is directly connected and ccuu should not allow
           the driver to block waiting for a carrier to be detected.

     --EE _e_s_c_a_p_e___c_h_a_r
           Specify an escape character to use instead of the default tilde.

     --ll _l_i_n_e
           Specify the line to use.  Any of the forms _c_u_a_0_0, _/_d_e_v_/_c_u_a_0_0, or
           _u_s_b_0_._1_._0_0_0_0_2_._3 are permitted.

           The default is _/_d_e_v_/_c_u_a_0_0.  See cua(4) for information on terminal
           devices.  Users in group “dialer” are permitted to use cua(4) de‐
           vices by default.

           See sysctl(2) _h_w_._u_c_o_m_n_a_m_e_s for available USB serial lines.

     --rr    Start ccuu in restricted mode.  This prevents all local filesystem
           operations (~~RR, ~~XX, and ~~>>) and command executions (~~CC and ~~$$).

     --ss _s_p_e_e_d | --_s_p_e_e_d
           Set the speed of the connection.  The default is 9600.

     If _h_o_s_t is given, ccuu uses the remote(5) database to retrieve the ddcc
     (directly connected), ddvv (device) and bbrr (baud rate) capabilities for
     that host.  The ccuu utility ignores other capabilities found in that data‐
     base.

     Typed characters are normally transmitted directly to the remote machine
     (which does the echoing as well).  A tilde (‘~’) appearing as the first
     character of a line is an escape signal; the following are recognized:

           ~~^^DD or ~~..   Drop the connection and exit.  Only the connection is
                       dropped – the login session is not terminated.

           ~~>>          Copy file from local to remote.  ccuu prompts for the
                       name of a local file to transmit.

           ~~$$          Pipe the output from a local UNIX process to the remote
                       host.  The command string sent to the local UNIX system
                       is processed by the shell.

           ~~##          Send a BREAK to the remote system.

           ~~^^ZZ         Stop ccuu (only available with job control).

           ~~CC          Fork a child process on the local system to perform
                       special protocols such as XMODEM.  The child program
                       will be run with the following arrangement of file de‐
                       scriptors:

                             0 ↔ remote tty in
                             1 ↔ remote tty out
                             2 ↔ local tty stderr

           ~~DD          Deassert the data terminal ready (DTR) line briefly.

           ~~RR          Record all output from the remote system to a file.  If
                       the given file already exists, it is appended to.  If
                       no file is specified, any existing recording is
                       stopped.

           ~~SS          Change the speed of the connection.

           ~~XX          Send a file with the XMODEM protocol.

           ~~??          Get a summary of the tilde escapes.

     When ccuu prompts for an argument, for example during setup of a file
     transfer, the line typed may be edited with the standard erase and kill
     characters.  A null line in response to a prompt, or an interrupt, will
     abort the dialogue and return the user to the remote machine.

     ccuu guards against multiple users connecting to a remote system by opening
     modems and terminal lines with exclusive access.

EENNVVIIRROONNMMEENNTT
     HOST       The default value for _h_o_s_t if none is specified via the com‐
                mand line.

     REMOTE     A system description, or an absolute path to a remote(5) sys‐
                tem description database.

FFIILLEESS
     /etc/remote  host description file

EEXXIITT SSTTAATTUUSS
     The ccuu utility exits 0 on success, and >0 if an error occurs.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     sysctl(2), cua(4), remote(5)

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     The ccuu (“Call Unix”) command first appeared outside of Bell Labs in
     PWB/UNIX 1.0.  It was reimplemented as part of the ttiipp command in
     4.1cBSD.  The current version was written for OpenBSD 5.4.

